# Docs before code

Godot 4.7 (`config/features` includes `4.7`) with Dialogic 2. Game code is under `game/`. Leave `addons/dialogic/` unchanged unless the user explicitly asks to edit the plugin.

Before writing, editing, or debugging GDScript, scenes (`.tscn`), resources, or Dialogic files (`.dtl`, `.dch`, styles), read the matching official pages. Do this on every code change, including APIs that seem familiar.

1. Name the Godot class, signal, or node, and the Dialogic subsystem or event the change depends on.
2. Fetch those pages before proposing or applying code. Open the specific class or guide, not only the homepage:
   - Godot stable: https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/
   - Dialogic 2: https://docs.dialogic.pro/
3. Use the available doc tools (Context7, Firecrawl, or web fetch) to read the pages in the current turn.
4. Follow the documented API. When memory and the docs disagree, the docs win. If a page is missing or unclear, say so. Do not invent methods, signals, or Dialogic event syntax.

Example: before `Dialogic.start()`, open the Dialogic code-access and timeline pages on https://docs.dialogic.pro/ and confirm the method, arguments, and signals. Before `AnimationPlayer` or `Area2D`, open that class under https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/.

Answers that depend on engine or plugin behavior should cite the page that was read, even when no file changes.
